1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:04,690
A clean working tree, a passing test, a

2
00:00:04,690 --> 00:00:05,670
healthy service,

3
00:00:06,470 --> 00:00:09,910
three reassuring signals, and three different
questions.

4
00:00:10,690 --> 00:00:13,070
Before we move into the bigger projects, let's

5
00:00:13,070 --> 00:00:14,310
see whether you can connect them.

6
00:00:14,830 --> 00:00:17,950
We'll trace one real behavior through Cursor,
then

7
00:00:17,950 --> 00:00:19,650
work through four short cases.

8
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:22,350
The result is a practice plan you can

9
00:00:22,350 --> 00:00:25,050
explain, instead of a score you can brag
about.

10
00:00:27,483 --> 00:00:30,183
Open the assessment beside the course
workspace.

11
00:00:30,963 --> 00:00:33,703
Your job is to locate the implementation, find

12
00:00:33,703 --> 00:00:36,423
a test that challenges it, inspect the running

13
00:00:36,423 --> 00:00:38,203
service, and record the revision.

14
00:00:38,983 --> 00:00:41,663
Then decide what each observation leaves
unanswered.

15
00:00:42,403 --> 00:00:44,403
If something feels unfamiliar, good.

16
00:00:44,843 --> 00:00:46,643
You've found a concrete place to practice.

17
00:00:47,343 --> 00:00:49,783
Nobody gets better at debugging by pretending
the

18
00:00:49,783 --> 00:00:50,723
terminal was obvious.

19
00:00:53,083 --> 00:00:55,383
First, establish where we are.

20
00:00:56,203 --> 00:00:59,103
Git status shows main and no listed file
changes.

21
00:00:59,783 --> 00:01:02,163
Then we ask Git for the current commit
identifier.

22
00:01:02,883 --> 00:01:04,483
These commands describe the checkout.

23
00:01:05,063 --> 00:01:07,503
They don't prove the service has reloaded that
checkout.

24
00:01:07,883 --> 00:01:09,943
And a clean working tree doesn't prove the

25
00:01:09,943 --> 00:01:10,783
application works.

26
00:01:10,783 --> 00:01:13,443
Keep the revision in your notes so later

27
00:01:13,443 --> 00:01:14,703
evidence has an anchor.

28
00:01:17,017 --> 00:01:20,257
Now, locate the behavior without asking an
agent

29
00:01:20,257 --> 00:01:21,157
to rewrite it.

30
00:01:21,677 --> 00:01:24,177
I use Quick Open in this Linux workspace to

31
00:01:24,177 --> 00:01:25,197
open the handler file.

32
00:01:25,977 --> 00:01:28,737
Inside the exact health branch, the cache
directive

33
00:01:28,737 --> 00:01:31,797
is assigned before the method check and before
the probe.

34
00:01:32,497 --> 00:01:35,057
Follow the early return and the exception
path.

35
00:01:35,057 --> 00:01:38,357
Explain why both keep the header before you
move on.

36
00:01:40,783 --> 00:01:43,763
Next, open the health test file and find

37
00:01:43,763 --> 00:01:45,043
the healthy get case.

38
00:01:45,763 --> 00:01:48,003
Read the assertion for the response header,
but

39
00:01:48,003 --> 00:01:50,623
also inspect how the request reaches the
handler.

40
00:01:51,303 --> 00:01:54,283
This test starts its own server with
controlled dependencies.

41
00:01:54,883 --> 00:01:55,903
That's useful isolation.

42
00:01:56,643 --> 00:01:58,483
It also means the test isn't a request

43
00:01:58,483 --> 00:02:01,723
to the long-running operations hub service on
our usual port.

44
00:02:04,150 --> 00:02:05,710
Run that one named test.

45
00:02:05,710 --> 00:02:09,910
On this checkout, it reports one pass and zero
failures.

46
00:02:10,630 --> 00:02:12,650
The result tells us that this checked path

47
00:02:12,650 --> 00:02:15,030
satisfied these assertions in the test setup.

48
00:02:15,510 --> 00:02:17,250
It doesn't stand in for the other health

49
00:02:17,250 --> 00:02:18,750
paths or the complete suite.

50
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:21,510
Running one specific case helps you
investigate.

51
00:02:21,890 --> 00:02:24,670
Bring back the broader checks before you
integrate a change.

52
00:02:27,050 --> 00:02:29,930
Now make a separate request to the actual
service.

53
00:02:30,610 --> 00:02:33,070
We see a successful response and the no

54
00:02:33,070 --> 00:02:33,890
store directive.

55
00:02:34,510 --> 00:02:36,830
This is another piece of evidence from a

56
00:02:36,830 --> 00:02:38,270
different execution path.

57
00:02:38,810 --> 00:02:42,010
The response does not identify a git commit by
itself.

58
00:02:42,570 --> 00:02:45,190
In the previous lesson, we restarted the
service

59
00:02:45,190 --> 00:02:46,230
after the change.

60
00:02:46,730 --> 00:02:49,770
For your own run, record that startup context

61
00:02:49,770 --> 00:02:51,990
instead of guessing from a green response.

62
00:02:54,517 --> 00:02:57,837
Put the observations together in a short
report.

63
00:02:58,537 --> 00:03:03,317
Checkout revision, handler location, test
command and result,

64
00:03:03,957 --> 00:03:07,477
live request and result, then the remaining
limits.

65
00:03:08,377 --> 00:03:10,557
Notice how little prose this takes.

66
00:03:11,257 --> 00:03:13,777
A teammate should be able to repeat your

67
00:03:13,777 --> 00:03:17,697
check without reverse engineering your
conversation with Cursor.

68
00:03:18,557 --> 00:03:21,877
The report is a handoff artifact, not a

69
00:03:21,877 --> 00:03:22,657
victory speech.

70
00:03:25,317 --> 00:03:29,117
First case an agent says done tests pass

71
00:03:29,117 --> 00:03:32,157
but its answer contains no command output or

72
00:03:32,157 --> 00:03:34,237
revision What should happen next?

73
00:03:34,637 --> 00:03:37,017
Choose the action that improves the evidence
for

74
00:03:37,017 --> 00:03:39,597
the claimed result pause and give a reason

75
00:03:39,597 --> 00:03:43,097
before clicking We're assessing your next
engineering move

76
00:03:43,097 --> 00:03:45,197
not whether you can spot the answer with

77
00:03:45,197 --> 00:03:46,017
the longest label

78
00:03:54,517 --> 00:03:57,457
inspect the relevant diff, and obtain the
actual

79
00:03:57,457 --> 00:03:59,257
check results for the current change.

80
00:04:00,097 --> 00:04:02,377
A confident summary can point you toward
evidence,

81
00:04:02,757 --> 00:04:04,017
but it can't replace it.

82
00:04:04,797 --> 00:04:07,217
Starting another agent to repeat the same
reassurance

83
00:04:07,217 --> 00:04:08,337
doesn't close the gap.

84
00:04:08,977 --> 00:04:11,417
If this case was difficult, practice a bounded

85
00:04:11,417 --> 00:04:13,877
task with an explicit stopping point and a

86
00:04:13,877 --> 00:04:15,077
small completion report.

87
00:04:17,317 --> 00:04:18,297
Second case,

88
00:04:18,977 --> 00:04:22,397
a request fails with connection refused before
the

89
00:04:22,397 --> 00:04:24,137
test receives any response.

90
00:04:24,797 --> 00:04:27,577
Does that establish that the new response
header is wrong?

91
00:04:28,177 --> 00:04:28,377
No.

92
00:04:29,317 --> 00:04:32,137
Inspect the server lifecycle and test setup
first.

93
00:04:32,717 --> 00:04:35,257
Once a request can reach the intended handler,

94
00:04:35,617 --> 00:04:37,677
the header assertion becomes meaningful.

95
00:04:38,317 --> 00:04:39,637
The failure is real.

96
00:04:39,637 --> 00:04:43,537
The conclusion still has to match where
execution stopped.

97
00:04:46,050 --> 00:04:49,230
Third case, you edit the handler, save the

98
00:04:49,230 --> 00:04:52,130
file, and a request still shows the old
behavior.

99
00:04:52,750 --> 00:04:55,210
The process was started earlier without a
watcher.

100
00:04:55,850 --> 00:04:58,910
Before asking Cursor for a second
implementation, check

101
00:04:58,910 --> 00:05:01,630
which process is serving the request and
restart

102
00:05:01,630 --> 00:05:03,650
it through the project's documented command.

103
00:05:04,410 --> 00:05:05,970
Then repeat the request.

104
00:05:05,970 --> 00:05:09,750
a source file and a running process aren't the
same thing.

105
00:05:12,150 --> 00:05:12,870
Fourth case.

106
00:05:13,830 --> 00:05:16,690
A pull request has a successful check for

107
00:05:16,690 --> 00:05:17,670
an earlier commit.

108
00:05:18,430 --> 00:05:20,390
Another change was pushed afterward.

109
00:05:21,230 --> 00:05:24,250
Is that earlier result evidence for the new
revision?

110
00:05:24,870 --> 00:05:26,730
It tells you about the earlier revision.

111
00:05:27,710 --> 00:05:30,910
Inspect the check's commit identifier and
obtain the

112
00:05:30,910 --> 00:05:32,610
relevant checks for the current head.

113
00:05:32,990 --> 00:05:34,450
The branch name can stay the same while

114
00:05:34,450 --> 00:05:35,650
the code underneath it changes.

115
00:05:38,083 --> 00:05:41,083
The assessment records your first choices and
lets

116
00:05:41,083 --> 00:05:42,863
you revisit every explanation.

117
00:05:43,683 --> 00:05:45,643
Use the pattern to choose a practice focus.

118
00:05:46,463 --> 00:05:49,483
If navigating files or starting services was
difficult,

119
00:05:49,943 --> 00:05:52,083
begin with the workspace and editor modules.

120
00:05:52,883 --> 00:05:54,883
If you confused a setup failure with a

121
00:05:54,883 --> 00:05:58,303
behavior failure, revisit the small change
exercise and

122
00:05:58,303 --> 00:06:00,403
spend extra time on checks and debugging.

123
00:06:02,983 --> 00:06:06,623
If an agent's summary felt sufficient, focus
on

124
00:06:06,623 --> 00:06:10,703
modes, context, and explicit acceptance
criteria.

125
00:06:11,303 --> 00:06:14,203
If the revision mismatch was easy to miss,

126
00:06:14,643 --> 00:06:17,743
practice Git first, then the pull request and

127
00:06:17,743 --> 00:06:18,683
CI module.

128
00:06:19,263 --> 00:06:22,263
These are priorities within the course, not
permission

129
00:06:22,263 --> 00:06:23,763
to skip prerequisites.

130
00:06:24,463 --> 00:06:27,623
Everybody still builds the shared workspace
and follows

131
00:06:27,623 --> 00:06:31,583
the project dependencies before tackling the
advanced workflows.

132
00:06:34,050 --> 00:06:37,170
Now, do the hands-on part without copying my
narration.

133
00:06:37,990 --> 00:06:40,030
Trace head or a rejected method through the

134
00:06:40,030 --> 00:06:43,370
handler, find its assertions, and run the
relevant check.

135
00:06:44,010 --> 00:06:46,170
Record a live response and explain how its

136
00:06:46,170 --> 00:06:48,170
evidence differs from the controlled test.

137
00:06:48,710 --> 00:06:49,870
You can consult the notes.

138
00:06:50,470 --> 00:06:52,490
The useful test is whether you can produce

139
00:06:52,490 --> 00:06:54,030
and explain the trail yourself.

140
00:06:56,617 --> 00:06:59,697
Use three levels for each part of your report.

141
00:07:00,377 --> 00:07:01,537
Demonstrated independently,

142
00:07:02,217 --> 00:07:05,077
completed with a reference, or needs another
attempt.

143
00:07:05,877 --> 00:07:09,677
Attach the command, location, or result that
supports your choice.

144
00:07:10,557 --> 00:07:13,537
Don't average away a missing prerequisite with
points

145
00:07:13,537 --> 00:07:14,757
from an easier question.

146
00:07:15,457 --> 00:07:18,457
Choose one gap, practice it, and revisit the

147
00:07:18,457 --> 00:07:21,017
same task until you can explain your
decisions.

148
00:07:23,583 --> 00:07:26,743
You've finished the foundations when you can
connect

149
00:07:26,743 --> 00:07:30,783
a requirement to code, checks, runtime
evidence, and

150
00:07:30,783 --> 00:07:33,723
a recorded revision while keeping the limits
visible.

151
00:07:34,383 --> 00:07:37,423
Save your report and one specific practice
goal.

152
00:07:38,223 --> 00:07:41,323
Next, we'll build the reproducible workspace
that the

153
00:07:41,323 --> 00:07:42,783
larger projects depend on.

154
00:07:43,123 --> 00:07:44,723
Which step took the most effort to verify?

155
00:07:45,203 --> 00:07:47,483
That's your starting point and now you have a
way to work on it.
